If they've really got what it takes, it's going to take everything they've got.

2
00:09:51,111 --> 00:09:57,111
Fame is a drama, music film directed by Alan Parker.

3
00:14:46,666 --> 00:14:52,666
A chronicle of the lives of several teenagers who attend a New York
high school for students gifted in the performing arts.

4
00:19:42,222 --> 00:19:48,222
Coco, played by Irene Cara.

5
00:24:37,777 --> 00:24:43,777
Ralph, played by Barry Miller.

6
00:29:33,333 --> 00:29:39,333
Doris Finsecker, played by Maureen Teefy.

7
00:34:28,888 --> 00:34:34,888
Montgomery, played by Paul McCrane.

8
00:39:24,444 --> 00:39:30,444
Bruno, played by Lee Curreri.

9
00:44:20,000 --> 00:44:26,000
Leroy, played by Gene Anthony Ray.

10
00:49:15,555 --> 00:49:21,555
Fame is a 1980 American teen musical drama film directed by
Alan Parker and written by Christopher Gore.

11
00:54:11,111 --> 00:54:17,111
Set in New York City, it chronicles the lives and hardships of students attending The High School of
Performing Arts, from their auditions to their freshman, sophomore, junior, and senior years.

12
00:59:06,666 --> 00:59:12,666
Producer David De Silva conceived the premise in 1976,
partially inspired by the musical A Chorus Line.

13
01:04:02,222 --> 01:04:08,222
He commissioned playwright Gore to write the script, originally titled
Hot Lunch, before selling it to Metro-Goldwyn-Mayer (MGM).

14
01:08:57,777 --> 01:09:03,777
After he was hired to direct the film, Parker rewrote the script
with Gore, aiming for a darker and more dramatic tone.

15
01:13:53,333 --> 01:13:59,333
The script's subject matter received criticism by the New York Board of Education, which
prevented the production from filming in the actual High School of Performing Arts.

16
01:18:48,888 --> 01:18:54,888
The film was shot on location in New York City, with principal
photography beginning in July 1979 and concluding after 91 days.

17
01:23:44,444 --> 01:23:50,444
Parker encountered a difficult filming process,
which included conflicts with U.S.

18
01:28:40,000 --> 01:28:46,000
labor unions over various aspects of the film's production.

19
01:33:35,555 --> 01:33:41,555
Fame received a limited release beginning at the Ziegfeld Theatre in New York City on May 12,
1980, and had a wide release in the United States on June 20, by United Artists.

20
01:38:31,111 --> 01:38:37,111
The film grossed more than $42 million worldwide
against a production budget of $8.5 million.

21
01:43:26,666 --> 01:43:32,666
It initially received a mixed response from reviewers who praised the music, but criticized the
dramatic tone, pacing and direction although the film has been reappraised over the years.

22
01:48:22,222 --> 01:48:28,222
The film received several awards and nominations, including two Academy Awards for Best Original Song
("Fame") and Best Original Score, and a Golden Globe Award for Best Original Song ("Fame").

23
01:53:17,777 --> 01:53:23,777
Its success spawned a media franchise encompassing several television
series, a stage musical, and a remake released in 2009.

24
01:58:13,333 --> 01:58:19,333
In 2023, the film was selected for preservation in the United States National Film Registry by the
Library of Congress as being "culturally, historically, or aesthetically significant".

25
02:03:08,888 --> 02:03:14,888
The film explores themes of new york city, dance,
dance performance, musical, rivalry.

26
02:08:04,444 --> 02:08:10,444
The tone is emotional.
